# Domain modules

A domain's **source location is declared, not assumed**: `config/domains.php` is the registry, and `DomainManager` resolves every path (`domainPath`, `providerPath`, `apiRoutePath`, `testsPath`) through it. A domain either lives under `app/Domain/{Domain}/` or, when its entry carries `package`, inside a Composer path package under `packages/{slug}/`.

## Path packages (core domains)

- **Why:** a downstream project must be able to consume a core domain without forking this repo. A path package keeps the code in place while Composer installs it as a real dependency (`silverspoonmedia/platform`, `silverspoonmedia/navigation`, `silverspoonmedia/notification`, `silverspoonmedia/audit`, `silverspoonmedia/billing`, `silverspoonmedia/ai`).
- **The kernel is the platform package.** `silverspoonmedia/platform` ships `App\Support\*`, `App\Domain\Identity\*`, `App\Http\{Controllers,Requests,Resources,Middleware}`, `App\Repositories\*`, `App\Console\Commands\Domain\ListDomainsCommand` (registered by `KernelServiceProvider`), `App\Providers\{Encryption,Product,DomainAccess,Settings,Domain\Identity}*`, `App\Rules\*`, `App\Traits\*`, `App\Exceptions\*`, and its `autoload.files` loads `src/Support/Settings/helpers.php` (root `composer.json` must **not** load it again — double declaration is fatal). Every non-kernel package declares `"silverspoonmedia/platform": "^1.0"`. Code the packages import is edited in `packages/platform/src/`, not `app/Support/`; the host keeps only its four product-coupled overrides (`app/Support/Product/Services/{ProductAnalyticsService,ProductInsightService}.php`, `app/Support/ListActions/Builders/{HashBlocklistQueryRowActions,MicrostockCredentialQueryRowActions}.php`), `AppServiceProvider`, the product providers, and the domain console commands. Kernel migrations stay host-owned (`users`, `permission_tables`, `system_settings`, `product_*`, `identity_oauth_accounts`): the package ships models for a host-owned schema.
- **One package may carry more than two core domains.** A package is a distribution unit, not a taxonomy mirror: domains consumed together ship together. `silverspoonmedia/audit` = ActivityLog + ErrorLog, `silverspoonmedia/billing` = PaymentGateway + Currency + Service → one PSR-4 prefix, registry entry and API route file **per domain**, one `composer.json`, one provider, one `tests/` tree. Bundling never merges namespaces.
- **Namespace never changes.** A packaged domain keeps `App\Domain\{Domain}\*` mapping to `packages/{slug}/src/`. Callers do not learn a vendor namespace, and translation keys do not move.
- **Host overrides win.** Root `composer.json` declares `"App\\Domain\\Navigation\\": "app/Domain/Navigation/"` explicitly so Laravel's own autoloader entry precedes the package entry in the generated PSR-4 map. Dropping that line makes `app/Domain/Navigation/...` dead code that silently loses to the package. One prefix per domain in a bundle. The same rule keeps the kernel overrides alive: root re-declares `App\Support\`, `App\Http\`, `App\Repositories\`, `App\Traits\`, `App\Rules\`, `App\Exceptions\` and `Database\{Factories,Seeders}\` over `app/` and `database/`. Those empty host directories are deliberate override hooks, not leftovers — `PackageOverrideTest::test_kernel_override_prefixes_resolve_host_first()` writes a probe file per prefix and proves the host copy still wins, so the hook cannot rot into dead configuration.
- **Package contents:** `src/`, `routes/{domain}.php` — one per domain, no prefix or name, the host applies those (a single-domain package may use `routes/api.php`) — `config/`, `database/{migrations,seeders,factories}/`, `resources/lang/{locale}/**`, `tests/`. The provider is auto-discovered via `extra.laravel.providers`; it calls `mergeConfigFrom`, `loadMigrationsFrom`, `translator->getLoader()->addPath(...)`, binds contracts, and registers listeners/commands explicitly. Never re-register any of that in `bootstrap/*`.
- **Package tests run standalone.** Each package has a `Package:{slug}` suite in `phpunit.xml`, so its `tests/` runs alone; the full run covers them anyway, since PHPUnit runs every suite in the file. A directory may sit in exactly one suite — twice makes PHPUnit warn per file and exit non-zero on a green run. Every package test extends `App\Support\Testing\PackageTestCase` (Orchestra Testbench): kernel providers + the package's own provider, schema from `App\Support\Testing\KernelSchema` + the package's migrations, the package's registry-declared routes under `/api/v1` — nothing from `bootstrap/app.php`, `database/migrations` or `tests/**`. Rows are cleared with `truncateTables()` because the schema survives the process (`Identity\Feature\FeatureTestCase` does it in `setUp`/`tearDown` for a suite that owns real users). Host policy a package still needs must be reachable from the package: the API error envelope is `App\Support\Http\ApiExceptionEnvelope` (registered by `bootstrap/app.php` in the monolith, by `ApiExceptionEnvelopeServiceProvider` for a consumer) and "never redirect a guest to `route('login')`" is set by `PackageTestCase` because Testbench's builder default would throw. A test that needs a host-assembled route, a host fixture or a product row lives in `tests/Domain/Core/{Domain}/` and keeps `Tests\TestCase`. Each package ships `phpunit.xml.dist` + `composer test` (`@php vendor/bin/phpunit`); `composer test:standalone` (`scripts/run-standalone-package-suite.php`, CI job `standalone-packages`) proves the standalone path. `tests/Domain/Core/PackageTestSuiteTest.php` fails on a test not on that base (resolving each file's `use` imports before judging the parent), a `phpunit.xml.dist` missing a declared test dir, or a missing `test` script; `DependencyDirectionTest` fails a package test importing `Tests\Concerns\*`, `Database\Seeders\*` or a product domain.
- **Registry entry:** `'package' => 'silverspoonmedia/{slug}'`, `'path' => 'packages/{slug}/src'`, `'routes' => 'packages/{slug}/routes/{domain}.php'`, optional `'provider'`, optional `'web'` for a server-side web route. Bundled domains share `package`/`path`/`provider` and keep their own `routes`. `kind`/`locked`/`parent` are unaffected. **Registry order is route load order** — a domain whose file was split out of a sibling keeps the URI position it had before (Currency before Service in `silverspoonmedia/billing`), so never alphabetize the entries.
- **Config ownership is per file, not per package:** a published vendor config the host owns stays in the host (`config/activitylog.php`, Spatie's, carries `sensitive_properties.*`), while the domain's own config moves (`packages/audit/config/errorlog.php`). The kernel ships `spa-routes.php`, `localization.php`, `services.php` and `hcaptcha.php` because Navigation validates a menu `route` against the first, Identity stores `users.locale` against the second, and `discord.redirect` / `services.spa.url` / the captcha verify URL must resolve on a kernel-only install; `KernelServiceProvider` and `IdentityServiceProvider` merge them shallow, so the host copy still wins. `packages/billing/config/services.php` carries the `frankfurter` FX block the checkout converts through, merged by `BillingServiceProvider`; the host ships no copy. A product name inside `packages/platform/config/*` is a drift the `DomainUninstallManifestTest` reference scan reports.
- **Kernel config is a subset of host config, and that is a test.** `KernelConfigParityTest` asserts `spa-routes.menu`/`deny` kernel entries all exist in the host list (a product domain adds entries; the kernel may not invent one the host rejects), `localization.php` is the same document in both trees, every top-level `services` block the kernel ships exists on the host with the same keys, and `frankfurter` resolves from the billing merge. A kernel default that drifts ahead of the host boots clean and only fails on a surface a user touches, so the guard is the only thing standing between a scalar edit and a broken install. The consumer-side twin is `KernelConfigSelfSufficiencyTest` (`packages/platform/tests/…/Support/`): a host config directory is absent there, so the assertion is that the kernel ships its own groups and that `client-scaffold/config/{group}.php` publishes the rest.
- **Routes** load through `DomainManager::packageApiRoutePaths()` in `routes/api/v1.php` and `DomainManager::packageWebRoutePaths()` in `routes/web.php` (a packaged domain's `web` key, applied inside the `web` group); `config/*.php` stays in the package, so deleting it from the host is correct, not a regression (`setting_config('notifications.delivery')` is Tier B and unaffected).
- **Dependency direction:** a core package may import `App\Support\*` and `App\Domain\Identity\Models\{User,Role,Permission}`; the kernel imports a package only through a kernel-owned contract (`App\Support\Navigation\PresentedTreeEpoch`, `App\Support\Encryption\DecryptionAuditRecorder`, `App\Support\ErrorLogging\ExceptionRecorder`, `App\Support\ActivityLog\{SubjectAliasRegistrar,SubjectLabelRegistrar}`, `App\Support\Billing\{FulfillmentRegistry,FulfillmentContext}`, `App\Support\Notifications\Contracts\NotificationDispatcher`, `App\Support\Broadcasting\Contracts\ChannelRegistrar`, `App\Support\Encryption\Contracts\LegacyColumnMigrator`, `App\Support\Ai\AiUsageConsolidatorRegistry` + `App\Support\Ai\Contracts\{AiUsageLedger,AiUsageConsolidator}`), never a package interface, and only behind `app()->bound(...)` so a host without the package still boots. A core package never imports a product domain, and never another core package: a seam to a sibling is a kernel contract the *owning* package implements — `NavigationServiceProvider` registers `menu` on the audit package's `SubjectAliasRegistrar` and a `MenuSubjectLabelPresenter` on `SubjectLabelRegistrar`, and a product registers its `FulfillmentHandler` on the kernel `FulfillmentRegistry` that `silverspoonmedia/billing` consumes, or its ledger on the `AiUsageLedger` binding and `AiUsageConsolidatorRegistry` that `silverspoonmedia/ai` consumes. `tests/Domain/Core/DependencyDirectionTest.php` enforces all three halves — the kernel's burn-down list is a constant that stays **empty** (no allowlist to grow), and a non-kernel package that skips the `silverspoonmedia/platform` require fails too; adding a package import to a sibling, the kernel, or the package→product direction fails it.
- **Version pinning:** a path repository infers the version from the current git branch, so each package declares `"version"` explicitly and the host requires `"^1.0"` (never `"*"`; the only exemption is the VCS dependency `silverspoonmedia/pdf-tools`, pinned to a commit hash). `PackageManifestTest` fails when `composer.lock` disagrees with a package manifest, when a root requirement is not a caret range, or when a sibling requires the kernel at a different minor than its own `version`. A bump is `-Bump` + `composer update silverspoonmedia/* --no-scripts` in **one** commit; a selected-package release pins that one package's `version` in its own commit and publishes it alone, so `verify-mirrors` compares the newest tag with the pinned version package by package.
- **Distribution is one-way.** `packages/{slug}/` in this repo is the source; `github.com/silverspoonmedia/{slug}` is a read-only mirror produced by `scripts/publish-packages.ps1` (`git subtree split` + tag). Never commit to a mirror, and never delete `version` from the monolith copy — it is dropped only in the tagged mirror commit. Publishing again for a tag that already exists resyncs the mirror's `main` and replays the version-strip commit on top, leaving the tag on its original commit. Contract: [core-packages.md](../../docs/integration/core-packages.md#distribution).
- **Consumer contract.** A downstream project is a host with no product domain and no `packages/`. Four rules hold it together, and each has a guard: (1) `config/domains.php` is the mounting contract — core-only entries with `vendor/silverspoonmedia/{slug}/…` paths, in registry order, never a literal `require` in a loader (`packageApiRoutePaths()` also mounts `vendor/silverspoonmedia/platform/routes/kernel-*.php`, the kernel's cross-domain surface); (2) `composer.json` declares the root kernel PSR-4 prefixes ahead of the vendor copy — that is the consumer's override hook; (3) `App\Providers\KernelServiceProvider` is listed in `bootstrap/providers.php`, never auto-discovered, because it owns the bindings `AppServiceProvider` owns here and a second pass would discard a resolved seam registry; (4) the project publishes the host-owned config groups the kernel reads and cannot ship (`app`, `auth`, `jwt`, `permission`, `encryption`, `cors`, `mail`, `broadcasting`, `database`, `session`, `filesystems`, `lookup`, `oci`). Template: `client-scaffold/`, guarded by `tests/Domain/Core/ConsumerScaffoldTest.php` + `packages/platform/tests/.../KernelConfigSelfSufficiencyTest.php`, smoke-run by `scripts/run-consumer-scaffold-check.php` in CI — which also runs the scaffold's own `tests/` inside the copy, since those need the copy's `vendor/` and no monolith suite can execute them. **There is no git subtree** — `update-core.sh`, `push-core.sh` and `client-subtree.md` are deleted and must not return.
- Windows: `"symlink": true` produces a junction and works; if it ever fails, set `"symlink": false` and run `composer update silverspoonmedia/*` after every change under `packages/`.
- Packaged domains are still **platform core**: `domain:remove` refuses them, and their code leaves by editing the package, not by scaffolding.

## Registry axes (all domains)

- Three axes, never collapsed into one label: `kind` (`core` = foundation, `product` = sold surface), products only `locked` (whether `domain:remove` refuses), and the composition axis `parent` below. Core is additionally pinned by `DomainManager::PLATFORM_CORE` — flipping `kind` in config cannot make a core domain removable.
- **Composition axis `parent` (product only):** a **subproduct** declares the product domain it extends (`'parent' => 'Archivd'`; Microstock is the only one). It is a full product — own key, activation, RBAC, SKU, namespace, `uninstall.json` — **never** a third `kind` value. `DomainManager::parentOf()` / `childrenOf()` expose it; `domain:list` prints the `Parent` column; `domain:make --parent={Product}` registers one (the parent must already be a registered product); `domain:remove` refuses a parent while a registered child points at it, so a subproduct never outlives its base.
- **Never** run `domain:remove` on a core domain, or on a product while `locked => true`. Unlock is a deliberate `config/domains.php` edit; there is no `domain:unlock`.
- `domain:make` always scaffolds a **product** (locked by default) with `Http/{Controllers,Requests,Resources}`, `Services`, `Repositories`, `Models`, tests, `{Name}RoleSeeder`, and a starter `uninstall.json`; it never touches migrations. `domain:remove` deletes PHP/routes/tests/provider only — migrations stay unless `--purge-data` asks for them.
- **Product domains carry an uninstall manifest**: `app/Domain/{Name}/uninstall.json`, schema `domain-uninstall/v1`. It is the reviewed map of everything the domain touches — `delete` paths across backend/SPA/docs, `edits` (shared-file surgery from a closed op vocabulary), `data` (tables, migrations, RBAC, rows), and `ignore` (survivor references, each with a reason). It lives inside the domain because it dies with the domain.
- **Every file a product domain touches outside `app/Domain/{Name}/` is recorded in that domain's `uninstall.json` in the same task** — route/config/lang/SPA/docs file, `DatabaseSeeder` line, env key, `docs-coverage.json` entry. `DomainUninstallManifestTest` fails on an unlisted reference, so the manifest cannot drift. A `.gitignore`d machine-local override is not repo content: it is not scanned and not recorded, and the hand edit it still needs goes in `notes`.
- `domain:remove {Name}` reads the manifest: bare run prints the plan then confirms; `--dry-run` prints plan + unlisted references and changes nothing (allowed while locked); `--draft-manifest` writes a scanner draft and removes nothing; `--force` skips the prompt without skipping the `unresolved` check; `--purge-data` additionally drops the recorded tables/rows/RBAC and deletes the recorded migrations, and is refused unless `DestructiveDatabaseCommandGuard` allows it (`ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE_DB=true` outside sqlite/local). No `uninstall.json` → the structural behaviour plus an explicit warning.
- Sibling checkouts resolve as `dirname(base_path()).'/monolith-vue-workers'` and `'/docs'`; an absent sibling is reported **unverifiable** (never as clean) and its manifest section is skipped, never silently passed.
- Removal procedure for the AI that runs it: [.cursor/skills/domain-remove/SKILL.md](../../.cursor/skills/domain-remove/SKILL.md) — schema and op vocabulary stay in [docs/platform/reference-architecture.md](../../docs/platform/reference-architecture.md#domain-uninstall-manifest), the single owner.
- Namespaces: `App\Domain\{Domain}\Models|Services|Http\{Controllers,Requests,Resources}`. Shared infra stays `App\Support\*`, `App\Http\Middleware\*`, `App\Repositories\Eloquent\EloquentCrudRepository`.
- **Do not** import one domain's services into another. `App\Domain\Identity\Models\{User,Role,Permission}` is the sanctioned hub; anything else is explicit and documented.
- **Do not** conflate the module registry with the other axes that reuse `core`/`product`: RBAC `domain_kind` on Spatie roles/permissions (Identity only), per-user product activation (`product_activations` + `product.active`), per-domain visibility (`product_domain_visibilities` + `domain.access:{Domain}`), and `product_analytics` counters. Each has its own owner and its own gate.
- Product H-RBAC: add roles/permissions with `domain_kind = product` and globally unique names (`Billing Admin`, never a second `Admin`). Never overload or rename core roles.
- New product routes: gate with `domain.access:{Name}` **before** `product.active:{Name}` / `permission:`.
- Loader order is presentation only — core requires first, product last, in `routes/api/v1.php`, `bootstrap/providers.php`, and `config/domains.php`. It never changes URL paths or route names.
- Details: [docs/platform/architecture.md](../../docs/platform/architecture.md), [docs/platform/reference-architecture.md](../../docs/platform/reference-architecture.md).
